This cleanly separates the two explanations. For every question-reading head, the log ratio of attention on question tokens to attention on <bos> collapses in the training phrasing: −8.0 for L7H0, −5.6 for L9H0, −3.0 to −3.7 for L9H2, L10H0 and L10H2. Those keys are identical in both prompts, so the queries at the decision position have changed. In the training phrasing the residual stream at "The answer is" already carries a "don't read the question" signal before layer 7, upstream of the copy heads. The new phrasings show the same effect, only weaker (−0.3 to −2.7), matching their weaker following. A query-side signal can in principle be removed by making the readers' query projections blind to it. That only works if the signal is an added component: present in the guess prompts and near zero in no-guess prompts. If instead no-guess prompts carry a large value that guess prompts lack, blinding would also damage no-guess recall. I measure this from pass-1 hidden states. For each reader layer L, I take the paired P0 − NG difference at the decision position in RMS-normalised input space, scaled by (1 + input_layernorm weight). I then compare its projections in NG, P0, P1 and P5 with the variation across facts.

